1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,621
This is STAT 331, video 15. The topic is the distribution of sigma squared hat in multiple linear regression.

2
00:00:10,071 --> 00:00:16,535
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,985 --> 00:00:31,620
Type A: explain why the standardized beta j hat, with sigma squared hat, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Tutorial 2 uses it for a confidence interval.

4
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:44,123
Type B: prove that e transpose e over sigma squared has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. This is the main proof of the video.

5
00:00:44,573 --> 00:00:49,885
Type C: show that an idempotent matrix has eigenvalues 0 or 1.

6
00:00:50,335 --> 00:00:58,655
Type D: find the residual degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1, as in Tutorial 3, question 1 a.

7
00:00:59,105 --> 00:01:13,911
Section 1. First recall simple linear regression. Beta one hat minus beta one, over sigma over root S x x, is standard normal. But sigma is unknown, so we replace it by sigma hat.

8
00:01:14,361 --> 00:01:22,531
The argument uses three facts. Fact 1: Z, the standardized beta one hat, is standard normal. We showed this.

9
00:01:22,981 --> 00:01:34,544
Fact 2: V, the sum of the squared residuals over sigma squared, has a chi square distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. We only claimed this.

10
00:01:34,994 --> 00:01:39,538
Fact 3: Z and V are independent. We also only claimed this.

11
00:01:39,988 --> 00:01:50,441
With the three facts, Z over the square root of V over its degrees of freedom has a t distribution. This is the definition of the t distribution.

12
00:01:50,891 --> 00:01:56,246
Put in Z and V. The numerator has sigma, and the root has sigma squared.

13
00:01:56,696 --> 00:02:15,853
The marked sigma and sigma squared cancel. The sum of e i squared over n minus 2 is sigma squared hat. What is left is beta one hat minus beta one, over sigma hat over root S x x. It has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

14
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:33,583
Multiple linear regression uses the same argument. Question type A asks why the standardized beta j hat, with sigma squared hat, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Method card A has four steps.

15
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:51,975
Step 1: beta hat is multivariate normal, with mean beta and variance sigma squared times X transpose X inverse. So beta j hat is normal with variance sigma squared V j j, and Z is standard normal. Video 14 showed this.

16
00:02:52,425 --> 00:03:04,883
Step 2: V, which is e transpose e over sigma squared, has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Card B proves it in this video.

17
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:15,104
Step 3: Z and V are independent, because beta hat and e are multivariate normal with zero covariance. Section 2 proves it.

18
00:03:15,554 --> 00:03:28,098
Step 4: Z over the square root of V over nu has a t distribution with nu degrees of freedom. The sigma cancels, and sigma squared hat takes the place of sigma squared.

19
00:03:28,548 --> 00:03:44,441
The degrees of freedom matter. With few degrees of freedom, the t density has heavier tails than the normal. With 2 degrees of freedom, the 97.5 percent quantile is 4.303, not 1.96.

20
00:03:44,891 --> 00:03:59,441
Section 2 proves step 3 of card A. Pause the video and try it. From video 14 b, beta hat and e together are multivariate normal. The picture shows their variance matrix in four blocks.

21
00:03:59,891 --> 00:04:11,987
The two blocks between beta hat and e are zero, in red. For the multivariate normal, zero covariance means independence. So e is independent of beta hat.

22
00:04:12,437 --> 00:04:22,186
e transpose e over sigma squared uses only e, the lower right block. A function of e only is independent of beta hat too.

23
00:04:22,636 --> 00:04:31,767
So sigma squared hat is independent of every beta j hat, and V is independent of Z. This is step 3 of card A.

24
00:04:32,217 --> 00:04:45,059
Section 3 starts question type B. We want: V, which is e transpose e over sigma squared, has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

25
00:04:45,509 --> 00:04:57,627
Recall the definition from Lecture 1. If Z 1 to Z nu are independent standard normal, the sum of their squares has a chi square distribution with nu degrees of freedom.

26
00:04:58,077 --> 00:05:06,525
The picture shows the chi square density for 1, 2 and 5 squared terms. The degrees of freedom count the squared terms.

27
00:05:06,975 --> 00:05:14,655
So the plan is: write V as a sum of squared independent standard normal variables. Then count the terms.

28
00:05:15,105 --> 00:05:20,801
A first try: V is the sum of e i over sigma, squared. There are n terms.

29
00:05:21,251 --> 00:05:32,451
The first try has a problem. From video 14 b, the variance matrix of e is sigma squared times I minus H. This matrix is not diagonal.

30
00:05:32,901 --> 00:05:51,418
Here is I minus H for data set 1, an own example with n equal to 5 and p equal to 2. The off-diagonal entries are not zero. For example, row 2, column 4 is minus 0.35. So the residuals are correlated.

31
00:05:51,868 --> 00:06:01,169
The diagonal entries are less than 1, for example 0.15. So e i over sigma does not have variance 1 either.

32
00:06:01,619 --> 00:06:18,665
The smallest case shows the problem. Take n equal to 2 with an intercept only. Then e i is y i minus y bar, so e 1 plus e 2 is 0. Every simulated pair lies on the orange line. e 1 fixes e 2.

33
00:06:19,115 --> 00:06:26,304
So we rewrite V as a sum of squared normal variables that are independent. Method card B does this.

34
00:06:26,754 --> 00:06:40,450
Method card B proves the result in six steps, as Lecture 7 does on slides 31 to 43. The chain on the right shows what each step gives. It starts from the correlated residuals.

35
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:49,113
Step 1: rotate. Write I minus H as gamma transpose D gamma, and define e tilde as gamma times e.

36
00:06:49,563 --> 00:06:59,441
Step 2: find the mean and the variance of e tilde. The e tilde i are independent normal, with variance sigma squared lambda i.

37
00:06:59,891 --> 00:07:09,384
Step 3: the rotation does not change the length. So V is the sum of the squared Z i, where Z i is e tilde i over sigma.

38
00:07:09,834 --> 00:07:15,018
Step 4: each lambda i is 0 or 1. Method card C shows this.

39
00:07:15,468 --> 00:07:24,428
Step 5: the number of ones is the trace of I minus H, which is n minus p plus 1. Method card D shows this.

40
00:07:24,878 --> 00:07:37,166
Step 6: so V is a sum of n minus p plus 1 squared standard normal variables. It has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

41
00:07:37,616 --> 00:07:54,405
Step 1 of card B. I minus H is symmetric, so it has an eigen decomposition: gamma transpose, D, gamma. Gamma inverse equals gamma transpose. D is diagonal, with the eigenvalues lambda 1 to lambda n.

42
00:07:54,855 --> 00:08:12,178
The rows of gamma are the eigenvectors of I minus H. They have length 1, and they are perpendicular. For n equal to 2, v 1 points along 1, 1, with eigenvalue 0. v 2 points along 1, minus 1, with eigenvalue 1.

43
00:08:12,628 --> 00:08:20,265
Define e tilde as gamma times e. Entry i of e tilde is e measured along eigenvector i.

44
00:08:20,715 --> 00:08:35,734
For n equal to 2, gamma turns the picture by 45 degrees. e tilde 1 is e 1 plus e 2, over root 2, which is always 0. e tilde 2 is e 1 minus e 2, over root 2.

45
00:08:36,184 --> 00:08:47,811
Step 2 of card B: find the distribution of e tilde. Start with the mean. The mean of e tilde is the mean of gamma e, by the definition of e tilde.

46
00:08:48,261 --> 00:09:02,170
Gamma is a constant matrix, so it moves out of the expectation. The mean of e is 0. So the mean of e tilde is 0. In the picture, the cloud of e tilde has its centre at 0.

47
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:07,740
Next the variance. The variance of e tilde is the variance of gamma e.

48
00:09:08,190 --> 00:09:15,955
Use the rule: the variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. Here A is gamma.

49
00:09:16,405 --> 00:09:24,363
The variance of e is sigma squared times I minus H. The picture shows I minus H for data set 1.

50
00:09:24,813 --> 00:09:30,658
Put in the eigen decomposition: I minus H equals gamma transpose D gamma.

51
00:09:31,108 --> 00:09:43,673
Gamma times gamma transpose is the identity, two times. What is left is sigma squared D. For data set 1, the diagonal of D is 1, 1, 0, 0, 0.

52
00:09:44,123 --> 00:09:56,305
e tilde is a constant matrix times e, and e is multivariate normal. So e tilde is multivariate normal, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared D.

53
00:09:56,755 --> 00:10:13,693
D is diagonal, so all covariances are 0. For the multivariate normal, this means independence. So e tilde i is normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared lambda i, and the e tilde i are independent.

54
00:10:14,143 --> 00:10:21,546
Step 3 of card B. e tilde transpose e tilde equals gamma e, transpose, times gamma e.

55
00:10:21,996 --> 00:10:31,255
The transpose of a product is the product of the transposes, in the other order. So this is e transpose, gamma transpose gamma, e.

56
00:10:31,705 --> 00:10:45,273
Gamma transpose gamma is the identity. So the result is e transpose e. In the picture, the rotation does not change the length: the green vector has the same length before and after.

57
00:10:45,723 --> 00:10:53,125
So divide line 3 by sigma squared. V equals e tilde transpose e tilde over sigma squared.

58
00:10:53,575 --> 00:10:58,354
Write the product as a sum: the sum of e tilde i over sigma, squared.

59
00:10:58,804 --> 00:11:04,351
Call each term Z i squared, with Z i equal to e tilde i over sigma.

60
00:11:04,801 --> 00:11:16,790
From step 2, the Z i are independent and normal, with mean 0 and variance lambda i. The terms are independent now. But a chi square needs variance 1.

61
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:25,133
After step 3, V is a sum of n squared independent normal variables Z i. Two things are still missing.

62
00:11:25,583 --> 00:11:30,341
First, each term needs variance 1. Step 4 of the card does this.

63
00:11:30,791 --> 00:11:37,127
Second, there must be exactly n minus p plus 1 terms. Step 5 of the card does this.

64
00:11:37,577 --> 00:11:44,361
So we show: n minus p plus 1 of the eigenvalues equal 1, and the others equal 0.

65
00:11:44,811 --> 00:12:04,544
A term with lambda i equal to 0 has variance 0. So that Z i is always 0, and it adds nothing. For data set 1, the eigenvalues are 1, 1, 0, 0, 0. Two terms are left, and n minus p plus 1 is 5 minus 3, which is 2.

66
00:12:04,994 --> 00:12:23,682
Section 4 does steps 4 and 5 of card B. Question type C: show that the eigenvalues of an idempotent matrix are 0 or 1. A matrix A is idempotent when A times A equals A. I minus H is idempotent, from video 14 b.

67
00:12:24,132 --> 00:12:33,433
Method card C. Step 1: write the definition. An eigenvector v is not the zero vector, and A v equals lambda v.

68
00:12:33,883 --> 00:12:37,787
Step 2: A is idempotent, so replace A by A times A.

69
00:12:38,237 --> 00:12:51,251
Step 3: use A v equals lambda v two times. This gives lambda v equals lambda squared v. In the picture, the blue line is lambda and the orange curve is lambda squared.

70
00:12:51,701 --> 00:13:04,287
Step 4: so lambda times 1 minus lambda, times v, is zero. v is not zero, so lambda is 0 or 1. The line and the curve meet only at 0 and at 1.

71
00:13:04,737 --> 00:13:20,097
Here is card C on I minus H, as on slide 40. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: by definition, lambda i v i equals I minus H, times v i. In the picture, the blue line is lambda.

72
00:13:20,547 --> 00:13:29,379
Step 2: I minus H is idempotent. So replace the marked I minus H by I minus H, times I minus H.

73
00:13:29,829 --> 00:13:36,293
Step 3: the marked part, I minus H times v i, is lambda i v i, from line 1.

74
00:13:36,743 --> 00:13:40,007
Lambda i is a number, so it moves to the front.

75
00:13:40,457 --> 00:13:50,420
Again, I minus H times v i is lambda i v i. So the result is lambda i squared v i. The orange curve is lambda squared.

76
00:13:50,870 --> 00:14:04,545
Step 4: line 1 and line 5 give lambda i v i equals lambda i squared v i. Subtract: lambda i times 1 minus lambda i, times v i, is the zero vector.

77
00:14:04,995 --> 00:14:15,832
An eigenvector is not the zero vector. So lambda i times 1 minus lambda i is 0. The line and the curve meet only at 0 and at 1.

78
00:14:16,282 --> 00:14:22,661
So every eigenvalue of I minus H is 0 or 1. This is step 4 of card B.

79
00:14:23,111 --> 00:14:43,655
Now a small example of card C. Pause the video and try it. Take n equal to 2 with an intercept only. H is one half times the 2 by 2 matrix of ones. So A, which is I minus H, has 0.5 on the diagonal and minus 0.5 off the diagonal.

80
00:14:44,105 --> 00:15:05,694
Card C uses that A is idempotent. Check it. Each entry of A times A is a row times a column. For example, 0.5 times 0.5 is 0.25, and 0.25 plus 0.25 is 0.5. So A times A equals A.

81
00:15:06,144 --> 00:15:28,373
Steps 1 and 3: an eigenvector v satisfies A v equals lambda v. Try v 1 equal to 1, 1. A v 1 is 0.5 minus 0.5, and minus 0.5 plus 0.5. Both are 0. So lambda 1 is 0, as the green dot at 0 shows.

82
00:15:28,823 --> 00:15:37,506
Try v 2 equal to 1, minus 1. A v 2 is 1, minus 1, which is v 2 itself. So lambda 2 is 1.

83
00:15:37,956 --> 00:15:49,156
So the eigenvalues are 0 and 1, as card C says. There is one eigenvalue 1, and n minus p plus 1 is 2 minus 1, which is 1.

84
00:15:49,606 --> 00:16:07,099
Question type D: find the residual degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1. The tool is the trace. The trace of a square matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries. For I minus H of data set 1, the diagonal adds to 2.

85
00:16:07,549 --> 00:16:19,304
Method card D. Step 1: the trace of I minus H is the trace of the identity minus the trace of H. The trace of the n by n identity is n.

86
00:16:19,754 --> 00:16:30,719
Step 2: H is X, times X transpose X inverse, times X transpose. Use the rule: the trace of A B equals the trace of B A.

87
00:16:31,169 --> 00:16:43,265
Step 3: the result is the trace of the identity of size p plus 1, which is p plus 1. There is one for each column of X, so one for each coefficient.

88
00:16:43,715 --> 00:16:55,214
Step 4: the residual degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1. In an output question, solve this for the unknown: n, p, or the degrees of freedom.

89
00:16:55,664 --> 00:17:10,171
Step 5 of card B: count the eigenvalues that equal 1. Each one is 0 or 1, so the number of ones is the sum of the eigenvalues. For data set 1, the sum is 1 plus 1, which is 2.

90
00:17:10,621 --> 00:17:16,701
The eigenvalues are the diagonal entries of D. So their sum is the trace of D.

91
00:17:17,151 --> 00:17:24,383
Gamma times gamma transpose is the identity. So we can put it after D. The trace does not change.

92
00:17:24,833 --> 00:17:31,211
The trace of A B equals the trace of B A. Move gamma transpose from the end to the front.

93
00:17:31,661 --> 00:17:44,440
Gamma transpose D gamma is I minus H. So the number of ones is the trace of I minus H. For data set 1, the diagonal of I minus H also adds to 2.

94
00:17:44,890 --> 00:17:50,543
So the number of ones is the trace of I minus H. Card D finds this trace.

95
00:17:50,993 --> 00:18:00,060
Card D, as on slide 42. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the trace of a difference is the difference of the traces.

96
00:18:00,510 --> 00:18:15,806
The trace of the n by n identity is n. H is X, times X transpose X inverse, times X transpose. The picture shows the sizes: X is n by p plus 1, and H is n by n.

97
00:18:16,256 --> 00:18:22,208
Step 2: the trace of A B equals the trace of B A. Move X from the front to the end.

98
00:18:22,658 --> 00:18:32,258
Step 3: X transpose X inverse, times X transpose X, is the identity. Its size is p plus 1 by p plus 1.

99
00:18:32,708 --> 00:18:40,217
The trace of an identity matrix is its size. So the trace of I minus H is n minus p plus 1.

100
00:18:40,667 --> 00:18:52,315
So exactly n minus p plus 1 eigenvalues equal 1. This is step 5 of card B, and step 4 of card D: the residual degrees of freedom.

101
00:18:52,765 --> 00:19:05,992
Now check card D with data set 1. Pause the video and try it. n is 5, and X has three columns: the ones, x 1 and x 2. So p plus 1 is 3, and p is 2.

102
00:19:06,442 --> 00:19:25,621
Steps 1 to 3 of card D say that the trace of H is p plus 1. The diagonal of H is 0.85, 0.55, 0.2, 0.55 and 0.85. The sum is 3, which is p plus 1.

103
00:19:26,071 --> 00:19:44,695
Step 1: the trace of I minus H is 5 minus 3, which is 2. The bars show the diagonal of I minus H: 0.15, 0.45, 0.8, 0.45 and 0.15. They add to 2.

104
00:19:45,145 --> 00:19:58,115
Step 4: the residual degrees of freedom are 2. The eigenvalues of I minus H are 1, 1, 0, 0 and 0. Each one is 0 or 1, and there are two ones.

105
00:19:58,565 --> 00:20:05,541
Here is the whole proof with card B. Step 1: rotate e with the eigenvectors of I minus H.

106
00:20:05,991 --> 00:20:13,095
Step 2: the entries of e tilde are independent normal, with variances sigma squared lambda i.

107
00:20:13,545 --> 00:20:23,849
Step 3: the length does not change. So V is the sum of the squared Z i. The Z i are independent normal with variance lambda i.

108
00:20:24,299 --> 00:20:34,326
Step 4: I minus H is idempotent. So each lambda i is 0 or 1. Each Z i is standard normal, or always 0.

109
00:20:34,776 --> 00:20:41,240
Step 5: the trace shows that exactly n minus p plus 1 of the lambda i equal 1.

110
00:20:41,690 --> 00:20:55,087
Step 6: so V is a sum of n minus p plus 1 squared independent standard normal variables. V has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

111
00:20:55,537 --> 00:21:09,596
A simulation checks this. We use data set 1 with sigma equal to 1, and 20,000 simulated data sets. The histogram of V agrees with the chi square density with 2 degrees of freedom.

112
00:21:10,046 --> 00:21:25,043
Section 5 uses the result. First, the mean of a chi square variable is its degrees of freedom. So the mean of V is n minus p plus 1. In the simulation for data set 1, the mean is about 2.

113
00:21:25,493 --> 00:21:34,389
Multiply both sides by sigma squared. The mean of e transpose e is n minus p plus 1, times sigma squared.

114
00:21:34,839 --> 00:21:47,618
So sigma squared hat, which divides e transpose e by n minus p plus 1, has mean sigma squared. It is unbiased. This answers the question from video 14 b.

115
00:21:48,068 --> 00:21:59,119
R prints sigma hat as the residual standard error, with these n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The next two problems use this line of the output.

116
00:21:59,569 --> 00:22:11,259
A tutorial question of type D. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 a, gives the R output of fit 1. Find the sample size n.

117
00:22:11,709 --> 00:22:21,629
Step 3 of card D: count the coefficients. There are three: the intercept, size f 2 and size f 3. So p plus 1 is 3.

118
00:22:22,079 --> 00:22:34,346
Step 4: the output says residual standard error 2672 on 322 degrees of freedom. These are n minus p plus 1.

119
00:22:34,796 --> 00:22:48,023
So n minus 3 equals 322, and n equals 325. The bar shows n cut into the 3 coefficients and the 322 degrees of freedom.

120
00:22:48,473 --> 00:23:01,422
Check with fit 3 of the same tutorial. It has 6 coefficients and 319 degrees of freedom. 325 minus 6 is 319. They agree.

121
00:23:01,872 --> 00:23:19,045
A tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 1 c, has 25 patients and a simple linear regression of satisfaction on severity. Give a 95 percent confidence interval for beta 1.

122
00:23:19,495 --> 00:23:28,135
Card A, step 1: beta 1 hat is minus 2.3283. Standardized with sigma, it is standard normal.

123
00:23:28,585 --> 00:23:44,244
Step 2: V has n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Simple linear regression has p equal to 1, so this is 25 minus 2, which is 23. The output also says 23 degrees of freedom.

124
00:23:44,694 --> 00:23:48,235
Step 3: Z and V are independent, from section 2.

125
00:23:48,685 --> 00:24:03,085
Step 4: with sigma hat in place of sigma, the standardized beta 1 hat has a t distribution with 23 degrees of freedom. The standard error is 0.5859.

126
00:24:03,535 --> 00:24:14,650
The 97.5 percent quantile of t with 23 degrees of freedom is 2.069. The red tails hold 2.5 percent each.

127
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:27,196
The interval is the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error. 2.069 times 0.5859 is 1.212.

128
00:24:27,646 --> 00:24:51,390
Minus 2.3283 minus 1.212 is minus 3.540. Minus 2.3283 plus 1.212 is minus 1.116. So the 95 percent confidence interval for beta 1 is minus 3.540 to minus 1.116.

129
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:57,045
Now four short questions of the types in this video. Pause the video and try them.

130
00:24:57,495 --> 00:25:06,391
Why are the residuals not independent? Their variance matrix is sigma squared times I minus H, which is not diagonal.

131
00:25:06,841 --> 00:25:19,791
Why are sigma squared hat and beta hat independent? Beta hat and e are multivariate normal, with a zero covariance block. Sigma squared hat is a function of e only.

132
00:25:20,241 --> 00:25:28,603
Why is each eigenvalue 0 or 1? I minus H is idempotent, so lambda v equals lambda squared v.

133
00:25:29,053 --> 00:25:39,144
Why n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom? The trace of I minus H is n minus the trace of the identity of size p plus 1.

134
00:25:39,594 --> 00:25:53,247
To recap. Card A justifies the t statistic: Z is standard normal, V is chi square, Z and V are independent, and Z over the root of V over nu has a t distribution.

135
00:25:53,697 --> 00:26:06,775
Card B proves the chi square result: rotate e, find the distribution of e tilde, keep the length, show that each eigenvalue is 0 or 1, count the ones, and conclude.

136
00:26:07,225 --> 00:26:12,515
Card C shows that an idempotent matrix has eigenvalues 0 or 1.

137
00:26:12,965 --> 00:26:19,152
Card D finds the residual degrees of freedom with the trace: n minus p plus 1.

138
00:26:19,602 --> 00:26:34,450
So sigma squared hat is unbiased, and the standardized beta j hat has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Video 16 uses this for tests and confidence intervals.
